From 4af5e251244522d065e7fa01462aa97e85a8d055 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ronnie Sahlberg <ronniesahlberg@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 09:37:50 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 4/9] Validate untrusted lengths and offsets throughout the PDU
 decoders

Several decoders trusted length and offset fields taken straight off the
wire, which a malicious peer could use to drive out-of-bounds accesses.
All of these are reachable from a hostile SMB2 server against a client,
and the NTLMSSP and negotiate-context ones are reachable pre-auth from
any client against smb2_server.

socket.c: the session packet length was never validated at all. In the
SMB3 transform-header branch the payload buffer is sized as spl - 52 and
then 12 bytes are unconditionally copied into it, so an SPL of 52..63
gave a 12 byte heap write into a buffer of 0..11 bytes. This is reached
from the very first bytes read off the socket, before negotiate. Reject
an SPL that can not hold an SMB2 header or that exceeds SMB2_MAX_PDU_SIZE
(the ceiling this file already applies elsewhere; comfortably above the
1MB/8MB max_read_size real servers advertise), and check explicitly for
the 12 bytes in the transform branch. smb3-seal.c gets the same check for
the decrypted blob, which re-enters the state machine with enc_len
standing in for the SPL.

smb2-data-file-info.c: smb2_decode_file_stream_info() advanced by the
wire-supplied NextEntryOffset while writing one struct per iteration.
Callers size the array as 1 + vec->len / 24 entries, so a NextEntryOffset
below 24 (nothing required it to be >= 24) overran the allocation.
Require it to skip at least the fixed part of an entry and to stay in the
buffer, and check the fixed part is present before decoding it.

ntlmssp.c: ntlmssp_get_utf16_field() used the 32-bit field offset as a
raw index with no check, so the Domain/User/Workstation fields of an
AUTHENTICATE_MESSAGE read at an arbitrary offset. ntlmssp_authenticate_blob()
read the 64 byte fixed part while only requiring 8 bytes, allowed the NTLM
response offset to sit one past the end, and never bounded the response
length, so the "temp" blob was copied out of bounds. ntlm_decode_challenge_message()
validated the length but not the offset of the target-info block (unlike
the target-name block just above it), and the attribute walk ignored the
4 byte attribute header, letting inlen underflow. Bound all of them, and
bound the target-name we append to the output buffer.

asn1-ber.c/spnego-wrapper.c: a BER length was decoded without checking it
fits in the remaining input, and the SPNEGO unwrappers handed that length
back as the token length, so a short security buffer claiming a large
OCTET STRING made the NTLMSSP layer read past the end.

smb2-cmd-ioctl.c: the default branch of smb2_process_ioctl_variable()
allocated output_count bytes but copied iov->len - IOV_OFFSET_IOCTL, which
also covers the padded input buffer, overflowing whenever input_count was
non-zero. This is the path FSCTL_PIPE_TRANSCEIVE and therefore all DCERPC
traffic takes.

smb2-cmd-query-directory.c: four error paths freed the payload without
clearing pdu->payload, leaving a dangling pointer that smb2_free_pdu()
frees again during teardown. Every other command file already nulls it
first.

Also fixed while in here:
 - smb2_encode_negotiate_reply() sized the security buffer from the fixed
   part of the reply instead of from the security buffer, and read seclen
   uninitialized when there was no security buffer.
 - the negotiate context loops used type/len uninitialized at the buffer
   boundary and did not bound the context data; the NETNAME context was
   converted with no bounds check at all.
 - PreviousSessionId was decoded at offset 18 rather than 16, past the end
   of the 24 byte session setup request structure.
 - smb2_process_read_request_fixed() tested ReadChannelInfoLength before
   decoding it, so every read request carrying channel info was rejected.
 - the error reply byte_count was not bounded by the PDU.
 - decode_ace() left ad_len/raw_len covering the rest of the ACL rather
   than the ACE, and ignored a NULL sid.
 - several (uint16_t)smb2->spl truncating casts in bounds checks.
 - unchecked smb2_alloc_init()/malloc()/smb2_utf16_to_utf8() results in
   the query-info, ioctl, reparse point and NTLMSSP paths.

The three most severe issues were confirmed with AddressSanitizer before
and after: the transform-header write, the stream-info overrun and the
NTLMSSP field offset now all pass cleanly, and a well-formed stream-info
chain still decodes as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit bae5ef5d94537dcaf5fb520cfed22bb84c99e20b)
---
